What was copied, and from which files
  crates/orb-engine/src/tb303.rs
    The voice: oscillator, 18 dB filter, envelope, accent, glide, the
    2.0 block (`process_20`) and the Vintage block (`process_vintage`),
    note on, note off, silence, the live parameter write, and the
    model switch. The arithmetic is the upstream arithmetic, including
    the f64 evaluation and the f32 stores.
    Removed from that file before it was vendored: the 32 patterns, the
    step sequencer (`step`), SongDevice, ChannelDevice, song mode, and
    pattern load. Those functions are not compiled.
  crates/orb-engine/src/tables/mod.rs
    Only `tb303_pitch`, `tb303_filter`, `tb303_smoother`, and the
    private `smoother()` helper those three use, plus the constants and
    the f32 store (`st`) they use. The builders call libm's `pow`, `cos`
    and `exp`.

Transitive files that were reviewed and not copied
  The voice functions call only the three tables above and helpers in
  the same file (`square_n`, `clamp_c`, `st`, `Voice::sample`). They do
  not call the drum engines, the delay, the mixer, the distortion, the
  PCF filters, the sample player, or the sequencer.
  The table builders call only libm. They do not read Standard.rbm or
  any other asset.
  libm 0.2.16 is a normal dependency (MIT), pinned to the version
  OpenRB-338 pins, and is not vendored as source.

What was deliberately left out
  assets/Standard.rbm and every drum sample
  panel artwork and the orb-ui / orb-ui-art crates
  the sequencer, the song format, and the rbs / rbm crates
  mod decryption
  the global delay, the mixer, the compressor, and the distortion
  the PCF filters and the 808 / 909 engines and their tables
  orb-cli, orb-player, and orb-testkit

Adaptations (not in the upstream file)
  The pattern memory and the device traits are gone, so this crate
  cannot load a ReBirth song or run the original sequencer.
  Three table indexes are saturated so a value outside the upstream
  range cannot panic the audio thread. Inside the ranges the voice
  already produces, the index is the upstream index. The sites are
  marked ADAPT in src/voice.rs.
  ChipWave, outside this crate, maps notes onto this voice, turns the
  tracker effects 3C and 3D into slide and accent, trims the level, and
  resamples from the voice's 44100 Hz blocks to the device rate. That
  code is ChipWave's and is not part of OpenRB-338. It is not claimed
  to match any recording bit for bit. OpenRB-338's own bit-exact claim
  is about its full engine; this extract has not been checked against
  a ReBirth export.
  One mapping note: the voice glides when the previous note's slide
  flag is set (a 303 pattern puts the flag on the note you leave).
  ChipWave's 3C is on the note you land on, so ChipWave raises the flag
  before that note and clears it again. The glide arithmetic is unchanged.

